          "HD-25 is Denver's professional core — a college-educated, higher-income urban district where 50% of adults hold degrees and median household income reaches $95,235, well above the state average. The 65% Democratic result reflects deep alignment with the party's positioning on education, climate, and social policy. But this is not a ideologically locked constituency; it is an economically sophisticated group of voters who are evaluating on whether government is solving the material problems they face. At $95,235 income, households are still constrained by housing costs, childcare expenses, healthcare premiums, and the debt service on degrees that often made them college-educated in the first place. The 31.8% renter rate — substantially higher than the state average — means nearly a third of the district's voters have no asset hedge against cost-of-living increases and are particularly sensitive to whether housing policy, renter protections, and economic mobility are actually improving. A representative who loses connection with the lived economic reality of young professionals and renters in a high-cost metro area will find the 65% margin under pressure.",
