          "The 37% renter rate is the single most significant vulnerability in the district's political economy. With a median income of $76K and rents reflecting the San Diego metropolitan market, renter households are facing sustained affordability pressure that translates into material quality-of-life stress. A constituency where more than one-third of households are renting — and therefore acutely sensitive to housing costs, eviction risk, and the relationship between zoning policy and unit supply — is politically volatile territory for any incumbent or candidate who does not address housing with specificity and genuine policy commitment. Younger professionals who are renting and unable to afford homeownership at San Diego prices, and minority households concentrated in rental markets with less inherited wealth for down payments, form a swing constituency that can be mobilized by a credible housing affordability agenda. The multiethnic composition (57% white, 16% Black, 15% Hispanic, 7.6% Asian) adds another layer: maintaining coalition cohesion requires that each community's distinct economic and cultural priorities be acknowledged with specificity rather than absorbed into a unified message, and the renter plurality gives a challenger a concrete organizing issue that can peel off renters across racial lines if the incumbent is seen as insufficiently responsive to housing costs and supply."
